f Terminals or Honors, maybe Dragon Pung, etc.).  \n- Potential patterns if I can finish: Possibly **All Pungs** (if B1 pung + pair B8) \u2192 6 points, plus **Pung of Terminals/Honors** for J1, plus possible **Tile Hog** if W9 kong counts, plus maybe **Seat Wind** if I get F2 later \u2014 but I can\u2019t now.  \n\nBest discard now:  \n- Discarding **B8** seems poor because B8 can pair with my B1 to make pair later, and B8 has 3 left, good draw chance.  \n- Discarding **T4** or **T2** reduces flexibility for chow, but I might aim for pung-based hand (since I already have kong and pung).  \n- Discarding **B1** leaves me with two suits (Bamboo, Dots) \u2192 maybe \"One Voided Suit\" later if I drop all Characters (I have W9 kong but that\u2019s meld, not hand), so my hand tiles after discard would be T2, T4, B1, B8 \u2014 that\u2019s three suits (T, B, B actually same suit) so not voided suit yet.  \n\nBetter to keep B1 to try for pung (3 left in wall), so discard a T-suit tile.  \nBetween T2 and T4:  \nT4 unshown count = 2 left, T2 unshown = 1 left \u2014 slightly better to discard the one with fewer left? Actually, keeping tile with fewer left reduces chances to complete. Wait, if I keep T2 (1 left), almost impossible to get another for pung, but can only use for chow T2-3-4 (needs T3). T4 has 2 left, could make pung of T4 maybe. But pung of T4 is not terminals/honors \u2192 less value.  \nGiven meld already T2-3-4, making another chow T2-3-4 would be **Pure Double Chow** (1 point) \u2014 not huge, but possible.  \nI might aim for B1 pung + B8 pair, and keep T2/T4 to possibly make another chow from draw.  \n\nI\u2019ll discard **T4** to keep T2 for possible chow with T3 (4 left), keep both B1s, keep B8.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nKeep hand flexible for both B1 pung route (3 left in wall) and T2-T3-T4 chow route (T3 available). Discarding T4 rather than T2 because T4 has more copies left (2 vs 1) so less rare to regain if needed, but actually I might not need T4 since chow T2-3-4 is already in meld, making another identical chow is still possible with T2 and T3, discarding T4 reduces duplicate terminals? T4 is not terminal.  \nActually T4 is simple tile. Keeping T2 (only 1 left) is riskier if I need a pair later (though B1/B8 likely will pair).  \nI think better to keep the tile with more copies left: T4 (2) > T2 (1), so discard **T2**. Wait, but T2 needed for chow if I draw T3; but if I discard T4, I can still use T2 and T3 to form chow only if I draw T3 and keep T2.  \n\nBut check counts: unshown T3=4 left, good chance to draw T3. If I keep T2 and discard T4, then hand: T2, B1, B1, B8 \u2014 I can chow T2-3-4 if I draw T3 (and need T4 which is discarded) \u2014 No! Chow T2-3-4 needs T2, T3, T4. So if I discard T4, I cannot make T2-3-4 chow unless I draw both T3 and T4 later \u2014 unlikely.  \nIf I keep T4 and discard T2, hand: T4, B1, B1, B8 \u2014 to make chow with T4, I\u2019d need T3 and T5 for T3-4-5 chow (not same as meld chow), possible but not identical chow.  \n\nGiven I already have T2-3-4 chow meld, making another chow T2-3-4 gives Pure Double Chow (1 point). To make it, I need T2 and T3 and T4 \u2014 I have T2 and T4 in hand, need T3. So discard B8 or B1? No, discard a non-chow component.  \n\nSo safest: keep T2 and T4, discard B8 or B1? But then no pair possible unless B1 pairs. If I discard B8, hand: T2, T4, B1, B1 \u2014 can make B1 pung (needs 1 more B1) + pair (needs any of T2/T4 to pair) \u2014 but T2 unshown 1, T4 unshown 2, so possible. If I discard B1, hand: T2, T4, B1, B8 \u2014 can pair B8 with new B8, or B1 with new B1, and make chow T2-3-4.  \n\nI think best to keep both B1 (pung chance) and B8 (pair chance), discard one of T2/T4.  \nBetween T2 and T4:  \nI need both for identical chow, but if I discard one, identical chow impossible. Then I might as well discard the one with fewer left to minimize giving rare tile to others? T2 unshown=1 left, T4 unshown=2 left. Discard rarer one (T2) to keep more common T4 for possible other chow.  \n\n**Final decision:** discard T2.\n\n**Answer:** Play T2"}
